  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>A manager chooses an inspection app carefully. She compares templates, weighs the price, sits through the demo, runs the trial. She picks a good one. Six weeks later her team has quietly gone back to paper, or to nothing, and the licence is money spent on a habit that never formed. This happens constantly, and it is almost never because the app was bad. It is because of something the buying process cannot see.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The argument, briefly","html":"<ul><li>Field software fails on adoption far more often than on features. The tool works; the team does not use it.</li><li>The real barrier is not price, functionality or training. It is the small act of opening a separate app and logging in, at the point of work, when hands are full.</li><li>That friction is invisible in a demo, where everyone is sitting down and paying attention, and decisive on site, where nobody is.</li><li>A better or cheaper app does not fix an adoption problem, because the friction was never the app's quality.</li><li>Removing the app entirely, by running the workflow in a tool the team already lives in, removes the barrier rather than lowering it.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The wrong diagnosis","heading":"It is not the features, and not the price","html":"<p>When an app goes unused, the firm reaches for familiar explanations. Perhaps it lacked a feature. Perhaps it cost too much to roll out fully. Perhaps the team needed more training. So the firm buys a different app, or pays for more onboarding, and six weeks later arrives at the same place. The explanations are wrong, which is why the remedies fail.</p><p>You can see they are wrong by looking at who does adopt. The apps that stick are not the most featureful or the cheapest. They are the ones people can be bothered to open. Feature lists are compared by the buyer, at a desk, in a considered mood. Adoption is decided by the worker, on site, in a hurry, with a job in one hand. Those are two different people making two different decisions, and the buying process only ever hears from the first.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The real barrier","heading":"The cost of opening a separate thing","html":"<p>The actual obstacle is tiny, which is exactly why it is so hard to beat. To use a dedicated app, a worker has to stop what they are doing, find the app, open it, wait for it to load, remember which login it wants, get past the password, navigate to the right form, and only then begin. Each of those steps is trivial. Together, at the point of work, with hands full and attention elsewhere, they are just enough to lose to. So the check gets deferred to later, and later means the desk, and the desk means memory, and the record decays.</p><p>This friction is perfectly invisible during evaluation. In a demo everyone is seated, unhurried and attentive, so the app opens smoothly and the friction simply does not appear. It only shows up in the one setting the buyer never observes: a real worker, mid-task, deciding in half a second whether this is worth stopping for. Most of the time, quietly, it is not.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Why more of the same fails","heading":"You cannot fix adoption with a better app","html":"<p>Once the barrier is named, the standard remedies are visibly beside the point. A cheaper app is still an app to open. A more powerful app is still an app to open, now with more screens to navigate. More training teaches people how to use a thing they are choosing not to open, which is a different problem entirely. Every one of these keeps the app and tries to compensate around it, and every one loses to the same half-second decision.</p><p>The only remedy that addresses the actual cause is to remove the separate app. Not to improve it, not to subsidise it, but to run the workflow somewhere the worker already is, so there is nothing extra to open and nothing to log in to. That is not a marginal improvement on the app model. It is a refusal to play the game the app model loses.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The medium is the decision","heading":"Meet people where they already are","html":"<p>Almost everyone doing a field job already has one messaging app open all day, and uses it without thinking: WhatsApp. It is already installed, already logged in, already the reflex for a quick message or a photo. Running the workflow there changes the physics of the decision. There is no app to find, no password to recall, no loading screen to wait through. A prompt arrives in the same chat the worker already glances at between tasks, and answering it is the same motion as replying to a colleague: a line of text, a voice note, a photo.</p><p>This is the whole design of Quickler, and it is a deliberately unglamorous one. Quickler does not try to build a better inspection app. It removes the app. The compliance workflow runs in WhatsApp, so the friction that quietly kills adoption is not reduced but eliminated, because there is no separate thing to open in the first place. The trade-off is honest: a dedicated form builder offers more configurability, and if your team will happily open an app, that configurability may be worth it. But if adoption is where your last tool died, the answer is not another app. It is no app.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>Our last inspection app was good, so why did the team stop using it?</h3><p>Almost always because of the small friction of opening a separate app and logging in at the point of work, not because the app was poor. That friction is invisible in a demo and decisive on site. A good app with an adoption problem is extremely common, and buying a different app rarely solves it.</p><h3>Would more training fix adoption?</h3><p>Rarely. Training teaches people to use a tool they are already choosing not to open. If the barrier is the act of opening a separate app when hands are full, more training does not touch it. Removing the separate app does.</p><h3>How is running it in WhatsApp different?</h3><p>WhatsApp is already installed, already logged in and already a daily reflex for most field workers. Answering a prompt there is the same motion as replying to a colleague, so there is no app to find, no password to recall and no loading screen. The friction that defers a check to later is removed rather than reduced.</p><h3>What is the trade-off?</h3><p>Quickler is not a drag-and-drop form builder with thousands of templates. A dedicated app offers more configurability, and if your team will reliably open an app, that may suit you. Quickler's advantage is adoption: if your team already uses WhatsApp all day, the check actually gets done. Quickler is also not a care records or clinical system; it sits alongside one.</p><h3>Related reading</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/the-report-written-from-memory.html\">The report written from memory</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/paperless-inspections-uk-trades.html\">Paperless inspections</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/inspection-app-uk-no-training.html\">The inspection app your team will actually use</a></li></ul>"}
